//
// A RECORD A STORED REFERENCE NAMES LIVES AS LONG AS ITS HOLDER, HOWEVER IT WAS BUILT
// (`@FR-H-RefHome`, @C145).  A holder in an enclosing block keeps the record its reference names (the
// local's store moves out to the holder's block); a call result built inside a literal argument
// lives as long as the record that holds it; and where no variable of the frame can own the record,
// it is built in its holder's store.  Three sections, each with its cells and receipt.
//
// ── a-record-a-stored-reference-names-lives-as-long-as-its-holder ──
//
// `@FR-H-RefHome`, `@FR-H-Removed`, `@C145` — a holder declared in an enclosing block keeps the
// record its stored reference names: the named local holding the record has its store moved
// out to the holder's block.  A local refilled by a loop pass removes the records of the pass
// before, so a reference still naming one reads null and the holder re-pointed that pass reads
// the new record.  After each cell a three-element vector is built, so a freed store reused
// for it would show up as 777, 778 or 779.
//
// - INNER BLOCK: a `vector` (21), a `sorted` (31), a `hash` (41) and an `index` (45) local of
//   an `if` block, one two `if`s deep (6), one of a bare block (9), the local's own record (5),
//   a holder inside the block and one outside it naming the same record (8 8), and a holder of
//   the block copied out to the outer one (3).
// - LOOP PASS: the holder re-pointed every pass (11), an element of an outer collection as the
//   holder (51), a callee linking a loop-local record into it (61) and the same through a
//   second callee (71); a second holder kept from the first pass reads null (-1 11, -1 61), as
//   does the reference read before the next pass re-points it (-1 -1 11), and the same for a
//   `hash` (-1 41), a `sorted` (-1 31) and an `index` local (-1 46), for a `while` loop (-1 3)
//   and for a local of an `if` inside the loop (-1 2); a holder inside the loop names its own
//   pass's record (0 1 2).
//
// Before, each of these compiled to a reference into a store freed with the block — the
// INNER BLOCK cells read 778 or a garbage integer — or was refused.
//
// @falsified-at: 278247d18 — interpret exit 1 -> 0, native exit 1 -> 0
// @falsified-by: tests/falsified/a-record-a-stored-reference-names-lives-as-long-as-its-holder.patch — interpret exit 1 -> 0, native exit 1 -> 0
//   CHANNEL: the exit — the control refuses the loop and inner-block shapes as outliving their
//   record, so the program does not compile.
//   WITNESS: with the analysis withheld on this build (`LOFT_NO_REF_HOME=1`) ten cells fail —
//   the INNER BLOCK cells read 778 or a garbage integer and the callee cells 777 — and with the
//   tombstones withheld (`LOFT_NO_TOMBSTONE=1`) the seven cells holding a record of an earlier pass read
//   the next pass's record (11 for -1).
//   HOLDS: leak, panic and expectations are equal on both trees; only the exit moves.
//
// ── a-call-result-in-a-literal-argument-lives-as-long-as-its-holder ──
//
// `@FR-H-RefHome` — A CALL RESULT IN A LITERAL ARGUMENT LIVES AS LONG AS ITS HOLDER.
// `readk(Linked { link: mk(9), n: 1 })` builds the `Linked` record in a work-ref inside the
// argument, and `mk(9)`'s record — the one the stored reference `link` names — is a call result
// the scope pass gives a temporary home.  That home was scoped to the literal's block, so its
// free ran at the block's end, before the call: `readk` read a released store.  On the
// interpreter that read answered whatever the slot held next (`readk_alloc` mints first, and
// read 70 where 90 is right), on the C backend strict stores failed.  The home now lasts as
// long as the holder.  The other spellings were right before and are the controls: a literal
// bound to a local, one built each pass, a vector of holders, and a field write.
//
// Expected values by hand: mk(i) has k = 10 * i, so link.k + n is 90 + 1 = 91; three passes of
// i = 0, 1, 2 with n = 1 sum (0 + 1) + (10 + 1) + (20 + 1) = 33; `readk_alloc` subtracts the
// 70 of the record it mints itself (7 * 10) and answers the same 91 and 33; the field write
// re-points `link` from q (k 1) to mk(9), so 90 + 0 = 90.
//
// @falsified-at: `LOFT_NO_REF_HOME_LIFT=1` — interpret answered 71 in `argument_alloc` (the
//   first assert: `link.k` read 70, the record `readk_alloc` minted into the freed slot), and
//   native-c failed strict stores with 2 use-after-free reads.
// CHANNEL: the values by assertion and the strict-store use-after-free, both backends.
// HOLDS: `bound`, `passes`, `holders`, `field`, `passes_alloc` under the switch.
//
// ── a-record-built-for-a-stored-reference-lives-in-its-holders-store ──
//
// @observes: LOFT_NO_LITERAL_EXIT_BUFFER — the switch stops a callee building its literal into the record it is offered, so a call result is no longer placed in its holder's store and the call-result cells are refused instead.
//
// `@FR-H-RefHome`, `@C145` — a record a stored `reference<T>` names lives as long as the store
// of its holder (@PLN189).  Where no variable of the frame can own the record, it is built in
// the holder's store, so it lasts as long as the holder does.  After each cell a three-element
// vector is built, so a freed store reused for it would show up as 777, 778 or 779.
//
// - A LITERAL written straight into a holder that outlives it: built in a loop into an outer
//   collection (10 11 12), assigned into its elements (20 21), into a `vector` local's (40 41),
//   into a keyed element (50 51), into a parameter's elements (70 71), into a parameter (5), a
//   returned holder (6), one holder re-pointed a thousand times (999), three holders of one
//   loop (1 100 101 201), a literal holding a nested record and a text (60 61 q0 q1) or a
//   vector ([1,2,10] …), a float, an enum and a boolean (1.5 2.5 Red Blue), under an `if` arm
//   (100 102), beside a literal of the same function that needs no placement (1 80
//   81), and a write a callee makes on one call only (-1 9).
// - A CALL RESULT from a callee that builds a literal into the record it is offered: assigned
//   in a loop (30 31), from a callee with two literal exits (30 31 320), built in (10 11 12),
//   into a parameter (8) and a returned holder (9), and a record holding a nested record and a
//   text (60 61 q0 q1).
// - A REFILLED LOCAL'S OWN RECORD (`for … { p = P {…}; h.r = p }`), built in the holder's store:
//   the last pass's (1), a second holder of the same block set in the first pass (1 0), and
//   a holder declared in the loop (0 1 2).
// - A PARAMETER'S RECORD copied out of its home before the home is freed: a `vector` local's
//   element (2), with a write through the local after the link showing through the parameter
//   in and after the call (9 9), the local's own record (55), a `hash`, `sorted` and `index`
//   local's (41 31 45), a home of a loop pass or an `if` (12 77), a returned holder (1), one
//   copied into a returned record (5) or by `h = t` (6), two fields of one parameter naming
//   one record share one copy (70 70), and nothing is copied for a holder re-pointed to the
//   caller's record (90) or to null (-1).
//
// @falsified-at: 87f132c87 — interpret exit 1 -> 0, native exit 1 -> 0
//   CHANNEL: the exit — the control refuses every shape above as outliving its record, so the
//   program does not compile.
//   WITNESS: with the analysis withheld on this build (`LOFT_NO_REF_HOME=1`) 24 cells fail on
//   the interpreter and 25 natively: a loop's holders all read the last pass's record (12 12 12,
//   21 21, 51 51), and a parameter's or returned holder reads a freed store (777, -1, or a
//   garbage integer).
//   HOLDS: leak, panic and expectations are equal on both trees; only the exit moves.

struct P { k: integer }
struct H { r: reference<P>? }
struct K { k: integer, v: integer }
struct HK { r: reference<K>? }
struct Bag { items: vector<P>, hs: vector<H> }
fn link(h: H, b: Bag) { h.r = b.items[0]; }
fn relay(h: H, b: Bag) { link(h, b); }
fn noise() -> integer { t: vector<P> = [P { k: 777 }, P { k: 778 }, P { k: 779 }]; t[0].k + t[1].k }
fn rk(h: H) -> integer { if h.r == null { -1 } else { h.r.k } }
fn rv(h: HK) -> integer { if h.r == null { -1 } else { h.r.v } }

fn vector_of_an_inner_block() {
  h = H {};
  if h.r == null { items: vector<P> = [P { k: 20 }, P { k: 21 }]; h.r = items[1]; }
  assert(noise() == 1555 && rk(h) == 21, "vector: {rk(h)}");
}

fn sorted_of_an_inner_block() {
  h = HK {};
  if h.r == null { s: sorted<K[k]> = [K { k: 1, v: 31 }]; h.r = s[1]; }
  assert(noise() == 1555 && rv(h) == 31, "sorted: {rv(h)}");
}

fn hash_of_an_inner_block() {
  h = HK {};
  if h.r == null { s: hash<K[k]> = [K { k: 1, v: 41 }]; h.r = s[1]; }
  assert(noise() == 1555 && rv(h) == 41, "hash: {rv(h)}");
}

fn two_blocks_deep() {
  h = H {};
  if h.r == null { if noise() > 0 { items: vector<P> = [P { k: 5 }, P { k: 6 }]; h.r = items[1]; } }
  assert(noise() == 1555 && rk(h) == 6, "two deep: {rk(h)}");
}

fn the_locals_own_record() {
  h = H {};
  if h.r == null { p = P { k: 5 }; h.r = p; }
  assert(noise() == 1555 && rk(h) == 5, "own record: {rk(h)}");
}

fn an_inner_and_an_outer_holder() {
  ho = H {};
  r = 0;
  if ho.r == null {
    hi = H {};
    if hi.r == null { items: vector<P> = [P { k: 7 }, P { k: 8 }]; hi.r = items[1]; ho.r = items[1]; }
    assert(noise() == 1555, "noise");
    r = rk(hi);
  }
  assert(noise() == 1555 && r == 8 && rk(ho) == 8, "inner and outer: {r} {rk(ho)}");
}

fn a_holder_copied_out() {
  h = H {};
  if h.r == null { items: vector<P> = [P { k: 3 }]; t = H { r: items[0] }; h = t; }
  assert(noise() == 1555 && rk(h) == 3, "copied out: {rk(h)}");
}

fn re_pointed_every_pass() {
  h = H {};
  for i in 0..2 { items: vector<P> = [P { k: 10 + i }]; h.r = items[0]; }
  assert(noise() == 1555 && rk(h) == 11, "re-pointed: {rk(h)}");
}

fn an_element_holder() {
  b = Bag { items: [], hs: [H {}] };
  for i in 0..2 { items: vector<P> = [P { k: 50 + i }]; b.hs[0].r = items[0]; }
  assert(noise() == 1555 && rk(b.hs[0]) == 51 && len(b.hs) == 1, "element: {rk(b.hs[0])}");
}

fn a_callee_links_two_arguments() {
  h = H {};
  for i in 0..2 { b = Bag { items: [P { k: 60 + i }], hs: [] }; link(h, b); }
  assert(noise() == 1555 && rk(h) == 61, "callee: {rk(h)}");
}

fn a_callee_hands_the_link_on() {
  h = H {};
  for i in 0..2 { b = Bag { items: [P { k: 70 + i }], hs: [] }; relay(h, b); }
  assert(noise() == 1555 && rk(h) == 71, "relay: {rk(h)}");
}

fn a_holder_kept_from_the_first_pass() {
  h0 = H {};
  h1 = H {};
  for i in 0..2 {
    items: vector<P> = [P { k: 10 + i }];
    if i == 0 { h0.r = items[0]; } else { h1.r = items[0]; }
  }
  assert(noise() == 1555 && rk(h0) == -1 && rk(h1) == 11, "kept: {rk(h0)} {rk(h1)}");
}

fn read_before_the_next_pass_re_points() {
  h = H {};
  s = "";
  for i in 0..2 { items: vector<P> = [P { k: 10 + i }, P { k: 90 + i }]; s += "{rk(h)} "; h.r = items[0]; }
  assert(noise() == 1555 && s == "-1 -1 " && rk(h) == 11, "read before: {s}{rk(h)}");
}

fn a_callee_holder_kept_from_the_first_pass() {
  h0 = H {};
  h1 = H {};
  for i in 0..2 {
    b = Bag { items: [P { k: 60 + i }], hs: [] };
    if i == 0 { link(h0, b); } else { link(h1, b); }
  }
  assert(noise() == 1555 && rk(h0) == -1 && rk(h1) == 61, "callee kept: {rk(h0)} {rk(h1)}");
}

fn a_hash_holder_kept_from_the_first_pass() {
  h0 = HK {};
  h1 = HK {};
  for i in 0..2 {
    s: hash<K[k]> = [K { k: 1, v: 40 + i }];
    if i == 0 { h0.r = s[1]; } else { h1.r = s[1]; }
  }
  assert(noise() == 1555 && rv(h0) == -1 && rv(h1) == 41, "hash kept: {rv(h0)} {rv(h1)}");
}

fn an_if_inside_the_loop() {
  h0 = H {};
  h = H {};
  for i in 0..3 {
    if i % 2 == 0 { items: vector<P> = [P { k: i }]; if i == 0 { h0.r = items[0]; } h.r = items[0]; }
  }
  assert(noise() == 1555 && rk(h0) == -1 && rk(h) == 2, "if in loop: {rk(h0)} {rk(h)}");
}

fn a_holder_inside_the_loop() {
  s = "";
  for i in 0..3 {
    h = H {};
    if i >= 0 { items: vector<P> = [P { k: i }]; h.r = items[0]; }
    assert(noise() == 1555, "noise");
    s += "{rk(h)} ";
  }
  assert(s == "0 1 2 ", "holder in loop: {s}");
}

fn index_of_an_inner_block() {
  h = HK {};
  if h.r == null { s: index<K[k]> = [K { k: 1, v: 45 }]; h.r = s[1]; }
  assert(noise() == 1555 && rv(h) == 45, "index: {rv(h)}");
}

fn a_bare_block() {
  h = H {};
  { items: vector<P> = [P { k: 9 }]; h.r = items[0]; }
  assert(noise() == 1555 && rk(h) == 9, "bare block: {rk(h)}");
}

fn keyed_holders_kept_from_the_first_pass() {
  h0 = HK {};
  h1 = HK {};
  g0 = HK {};
  g1 = HK {};
  for i in 0..2 {
    s: sorted<K[k]> = [K { k: 1, v: 30 + i }];
    x: index<K[k]> = [K { k: 1, v: 45 + i }];
    if i == 0 { h0.r = s[1]; g0.r = x[1]; } else { h1.r = s[1]; g1.r = x[1]; }
  }
  assert(noise() == 1555 && rv(h0) == -1 && rv(h1) == 31, "sorted kept: {rv(h0)} {rv(h1)}");
  assert(rv(g0) == -1 && rv(g1) == 46, "index kept: {rv(g0)} {rv(g1)}");
}

fn a_while_loop() {
  h0 = H {};
  h1 = H {};
  i = 0;
  while i < 2 {
    items: vector<P> = [P { k: 2 + i }];
    if i == 0 { h0.r = items[0]; } else { h1.r = items[0]; }
    i += 1;
  }
  assert(noise() == 1555 && rk(h0) == -1 && rk(h1) == 3, "while: {rk(h0)} {rk(h1)}");
}

struct Linked { link: reference<P>, n: integer }
fn mk(i: integer) -> P { P { k: 10 * i } }
fn readk(h: Linked) -> integer { h.link.k + h.n }
fn readk_alloc(h: Linked) -> integer { z = mk(7); h.link.k + h.n + z.k - 70 }

fn argument() -> integer { readk(Linked { link: mk(9), n: 1 }) }
fn argument_alloc() -> integer { readk_alloc(Linked { link: mk(9), n: 1 }) }
fn bound() -> integer {
  h = Linked { link: mk(9), n: 1 };
  readk(h)
}
fn passes() -> integer {
  t = 0;
  for i in 0..3 { t += readk(Linked { link: mk(i), n: 1 }); }
  t
}
fn passes_alloc() -> integer {
  t = 0;
  for i in 0..3 { t += readk_alloc(Linked { link: mk(i), n: 1 }); }
  t
}
fn holders() -> integer {
  hs: vector<Linked> = [];
  for i in 0..3 { hs += [Linked { link: mk(i), n: 1 }]; }
  t = 0;
  for h in hs { t += readk(h); }
  t
}
fn field() -> integer {
  q = P { k: 1 };
  h = Linked { link: q, n: 0 };
  h.link = mk(9);
  readk(h)
}

fn test_a_call_result_in_a_literal_argument_lives_as_long_as_its_holder() {
  assert(argument_alloc() == 91, "argument alloc {argument_alloc()}");
  assert(argument() == 91, "argument {argument()}");
  assert(bound() == 91, "bound {bound()}");
  assert(passes() == 33, "passes {passes()}");
  assert(passes_alloc() == 33, "passes alloc {passes_alloc()}");
  assert(holders() == 33, "holders {holders()}");
  assert(field() == 90, "field {field()}");
}

struct S3HK { k: integer, r: reference<P>? }
struct Q { k: integer, p: P, name: text }
struct HQ { r: reference<Q>? }
struct V { xs: vector<integer> }
struct HV { r: reference<V>? }
enum Color { Red, Green, Blue }
struct F { x: float, c: Color, on: boolean }
struct HF { r: reference<F>? }
struct HKV { r: reference<K>? }
struct H2 { a: reference<P>?, b: reference<P>? }
struct Out { h: H }
fn s3_mk(k: integer) -> P { P { k: k } }
fn mk_two(k: integer) -> P { if k > 31 { return P { k: k * 10 }; } P { k: k } }
fn mq(i: integer) -> Q { Q { k: i, p: P { k: 60 + i }, name: "q{i}" } }

fn a_literal_built_in_a_loop() {
  b = Bag { items: [], hs: [] };
  for i in 0..3 { b.hs += [H { r: P { k: 10 + i } }]; }
  assert(noise() == 1555 && len(b.hs) == 3, "built: {len(b.hs)}");
  assert(rk(b.hs[0]) == 10 && rk(b.hs[1]) == 11 && rk(b.hs[2]) == 12, "built: {rk(b.hs[0])} {rk(b.hs[1])} {rk(b.hs[2])}");
}

fn a_literal_assigned_in_a_loop() {
  b = Bag { items: [], hs: [H {}, H {}] };
  for i in 0..2 { b.hs[i].r = P { k: 20 + i }; }
  assert(noise() == 1555 && len(b.hs) == 2 && rk(b.hs[0]) == 20 && rk(b.hs[1]) == 21, "assigned: {rk(b.hs[0])} {rk(b.hs[1])}");
}

fn a_vector_local_holder() {
  v: vector<H> = [H {}, H {}];
  for i in 0..2 { v[i].r = P { k: 40 + i }; }
  assert(noise() == 1555 && len(v) == 2 && rk(v[0]) == 40 && rk(v[1]) == 41, "vector: {rk(v[0])} {rk(v[1])}");
}

fn a_keyed_element_holder() {
  m: hash<S3HK[k]> = [S3HK { k: 1 }, S3HK { k: 2 }];
  for i in 0..2 { m[i + 1].r = P { k: 50 + i }; }
  assert(noise() == 1555 && m[1].r.k == 50 && m[2].r.k == 51, "keyed: {m[1].r.k} {m[2].r.k}");
}

fn fill_elements(b: Bag) { for i in 0..2 { b.hs[i].r = P { k: 70 + i }; } }

fn a_parameters_elements() {
  b = Bag { items: [], hs: [H {}, H {}] };
  fill_elements(b);
  assert(noise() == 1555 && len(b.hs) == 2 && rk(b.hs[0]) == 70 && rk(b.hs[1]) == 71, "parameter elements: {rk(b.hs[0])} {rk(b.hs[1])}");
}

fn fill(h: H) { h.r = P { k: 5 }; }

fn a_parameter_holder() {
  h = H {};
  fill(h);
  assert(noise() == 1555 && rk(h) == 5, "parameter: {rk(h)}");
}

fn make() -> H { H { r: P { k: 6 } } }

fn a_returned_holder() {
  h = make();
  assert(noise() == 1555 && rk(h) == 6, "returned: {rk(h)}");
}

fn re_pointed_a_thousand_times() {
  h = H {};
  for i in 0..1000 { h.r = P { k: i }; }
  assert(noise() == 1555 && rk(h) == 999, "re-pointed: {rk(h)}");
}

fn three_holders_of_one_loop() {
  h = H {};
  g = H {};
  hs: vector<H> = [H {}, H {}];
  for i in 0..2 { h.r = P { k: i }; hs[i].r = P { k: i + 100 }; g.r = P { k: i + 200 }; }
  assert(noise() == 1555 && rk(h) == 1 && rk(hs[0]) == 100 && rk(hs[1]) == 101 && rk(g) == 201, "three: {rk(h)} {rk(hs[0])} {rk(hs[1])} {rk(g)}");
}

fn a_nested_record_and_a_text() {
  hs: vector<HQ> = [HQ {}, HQ {}];
  for i in 0..2 { hs[i].r = Q { k: i, p: P { k: 60 + i }, name: "q{i}" }; }
  assert(noise() == 1555 && hs[0].r.p.k == 60 && hs[1].r.p.k == 61, "nested: {hs[0].r.p.k} {hs[1].r.p.k}");
  assert(hs[0].r.name == "q0" && hs[1].r.name == "q1", "text: {hs[0].r.name} {hs[1].r.name}");
}

fn a_vector_inside_the_record() {
  hs: vector<HV> = [HV {}, HV {}, HV {}];
  for i in 0..3 { hs[i].r = V { xs: [1, 2, 10 + i] }; }
  assert(noise() == 1555 && "{hs[0].r.xs} {hs[1].r.xs} {hs[2].r.xs}" == "[1,2,10] [1,2,11] [1,2,12]", "vector inside: {hs[0].r.xs} {hs[2].r.xs}");
}

fn beside_a_literal_that_stays() {
  x = H { r: P { k: 1 } };
  b = Bag { items: [], hs: [] };
  for i in 0..2 { b.hs += [H { r: P { k: 80 + i } }]; }
  assert(noise() == 1555 && rk(x) == 1 && len(b.hs) == 2 && rk(b.hs[0]) == 80 && rk(b.hs[1]) == 81, "beside: {rk(x)} {rk(b.hs[0])} {rk(b.hs[1])}");
}

fn fill_when(h: H, k: integer) { if k > 0 { h.r = P { k: k }; } }

fn a_write_on_one_call_only() {
  h = H {};
  fill_when(h, 0);
  a = rk(h);
  fill_when(h, 9);
  assert(noise() == 1555 && a == -1 && rk(h) == 9, "one call: {a} {rk(h)}");
}

fn a_call_result_assigned_in_a_loop() {
  b = Bag { items: [], hs: [H {}, H {}] };
  for i in 0..2 { b.hs[i].r = s3_mk(30 + i); }
  assert(noise() == 1555 && len(b.hs) == 2 && rk(b.hs[0]) == 30 && rk(b.hs[1]) == 31, "call: {rk(b.hs[0])} {rk(b.hs[1])}");
}

fn a_callee_with_two_exits() {
  b = Bag { items: [], hs: [H {}, H {}, H {}] };
  for i in 0..3 { b.hs[i].r = mk_two(30 + i); }
  assert(noise() == 1555 && rk(b.hs[0]) == 30 && rk(b.hs[1]) == 31 && rk(b.hs[2]) == 320, "two exits: {rk(b.hs[0])} {rk(b.hs[1])} {rk(b.hs[2])}");
}

fn a_call_result_built_in() {
  b = Bag { items: [], hs: [] };
  for i in 0..3 { b.hs += [H { r: s3_mk(10 + i) }]; }
  assert(noise() == 1555 && len(b.hs) == 3, "call built: {len(b.hs)}");
  assert(rk(b.hs[0]) == 10 && rk(b.hs[1]) == 11 && rk(b.hs[2]) == 12, "call built: {rk(b.hs[0])} {rk(b.hs[1])} {rk(b.hs[2])}");
}

fn fill_call(h: H) { h.r = s3_mk(8); }
fn make_call() -> H { H { r: s3_mk(9) } }

fn a_call_result_into_a_parameter_and_a_returned_holder() {
  h = H {};
  fill_call(h);
  x = make_call();
  assert(noise() == 1555 && rk(h) == 8 && rk(x) == 9, "call holders: {rk(h)} {rk(x)}");
}

fn a_call_result_with_a_nested_record_and_a_text() {
  hs: vector<HQ> = [HQ {}, HQ {}];
  for i in 0..2 { hs[i].r = mq(i); }
  assert(noise() == 1555 && hs[0].r.p.k == 60 && hs[1].r.p.k == 61, "call nested: {hs[0].r.p.k} {hs[1].r.p.k}");
  assert(hs[0].r.name == "q0" && hs[1].r.name == "q1", "call text: {hs[0].r.name} {hs[1].r.name}");
}

fn own_record_of_a_loop_pass() {
  h = H {};
  for i in 0..2 { p = P { k: i }; h.r = p; }
  assert(noise() == 1555 && rk(h) == 1, "own: {rk(h)}");
}

fn own_record_with_a_second_holder() {
  h = H {};
  g = H {};
  for i in 0..2 { p = P { k: i }; h.r = p; if i == 0 { g.r = p; } }
  assert(noise() == 1555 && rk(h) == 1 && rk(g) == 0, "own second: {rk(h)} {rk(g)}");
}

fn own_record_with_the_holder_in_the_loop() {
  s = "";
  for i in 0..3 { h = H {}; p = P { k: i }; h.r = p; s += "{rk(h)} "; }
  assert(noise() == 1555 && s == "0 1 2 ", "own in loop: {s}");
}

fn copy_out_element(h: H) { items: vector<P> = [P { k: 1 }, P { k: 2 }]; h.r = items[1]; }
fn copy_out_after_a_write(h: H) -> integer {
  items: vector<P> = [P { k: 1 }, P { k: 2 }];
  h.r = items[1];
  items[1].k = 9;
  rk(h)
}
fn copy_out_own(h: H) { p = P { k: 55 }; h.r = p; }
fn copy_out_hash(h: HKV) { s: hash<K[k]> = [K { k: 1, v: 41 }]; h.r = s[1]; }
fn copy_out_sorted(h: HKV) { s: sorted<K[k]> = [K { k: 1, v: 31 }]; h.r = s[1]; }
fn copy_out_index(h: HKV) { s: index<K[k]> = [K { k: 1, v: 45 }]; h.r = s[1]; }
fn copy_out_per_pass(h: H) { for i in 0..3 { items: vector<P> = [P { k: 10 + i }]; h.r = items[0]; } }
fn copy_out_of_an_if(h: H) { if h.r == null { items: vector<P> = [P { k: 77 }]; h.r = items[0]; } }
fn copy_out_returned() -> H { items: vector<P> = [P { k: 1 }]; H { r: items[0] } }
fn copy_out_into_a_returned_record() -> Out { items: vector<P> = [P { k: 5 }]; h = H { r: items[0] }; Out { h: h } }
fn copy_out_assigned() -> H { items: vector<P> = [P { k: 6 }]; t = H { r: items[0] }; h = t; h }
fn copy_out_two_fields(h: H2) { items: vector<P> = [P { k: 7 }]; h.a = items[0]; h.b = items[0]; }
fn copy_out_re_pointed(h: H, q: Bag) { items: vector<P> = [P { k: 1 }]; h.r = items[0]; h.r = q.items[0]; }
fn copy_out_cleared(h: H) { items: vector<P> = [P { k: 1 }]; h.r = items[0]; h.r = null; }
fn s3_rv(h: HKV) -> integer { if h.r == null { -1 } else { h.r.v } }

fn a_parameters_record_copied_out() {
  a = H {};
  copy_out_element(a);
  b = H {};
  w = copy_out_after_a_write(b);
  c = H {};
  copy_out_own(c);
  assert(noise() == 1555 && rk(a) == 2 && w == 9 && rk(b) == 9 && rk(c) == 55, "copied: {rk(a)} {w} {rk(b)} {rk(c)}");
}

fn a_keyed_home_copied_out() {
  a = HKV {};
  b = HKV {};
  c = HKV {};
  copy_out_hash(a);
  copy_out_sorted(b);
  copy_out_index(c);
  assert(noise() == 1555 && s3_rv(a) == 41 && s3_rv(b) == 31 && s3_rv(c) == 45, "keyed: {s3_rv(a)} {s3_rv(b)} {s3_rv(c)}");
}

fn a_home_of_a_loop_or_an_if_copied_out() {
  a = H {};
  b = H {};
  copy_out_per_pass(a);
  copy_out_of_an_if(b);
  assert(noise() == 1555 && rk(a) == 12 && rk(b) == 77, "loop/if: {rk(a)} {rk(b)}");
}

fn a_returned_holder_copied_out() {
  a = copy_out_returned();
  o = copy_out_into_a_returned_record();
  b = copy_out_assigned();
  assert(noise() == 1555 && rk(a) == 1 && rk(o.h) == 5 && rk(b) == 6, "returned: {rk(a)} {rk(o.h)} {rk(b)}");
}

fn two_fields_share_one_copy() {
  h = H2 {};
  copy_out_two_fields(h);
  h.a.k = 70;
  assert(noise() == 1555 && h.a.k == 70 && h.b.k == 70, "shared: {h.a.k} {h.b.k}");
}

fn nothing_copied_for_another_record() {
  h = H {};
  q = Bag { items: [P { k: 90 }], hs: [] };
  copy_out_re_pointed(h, q);
  g = H {};
  copy_out_cleared(g);
  q.items[0].k = 91;
  assert(noise() == 1555 && rk(h) == 91 && rk(g) == -1, "not copied: {rk(h)} {rk(g)}");
}

fn a_float_an_enum_and_a_boolean_under_an_if() {
  b = Bag { items: [], hs: [H {}, H {}] };
  for i in 0..4 { if i % 2 == 0 { b.hs[i / 2].r = P { k: 100 + i }; } }
  hf: vector<HF> = [HF {}, HF {}];
  for i in 0..2 { hf[i].r = F { x: 1.5 + i, c: if i == 0 { Color.Red } else { Color.Blue }, on: i == 1 }; }
  assert(noise() == 1555 && rk(b.hs[0]) == 100 && rk(b.hs[1]) == 102, "if arm: {rk(b.hs[0])} {rk(b.hs[1])}");
  assert("{hf[0].r.x} {hf[1].r.x} {hf[0].r.c} {hf[1].r.c} {hf[0].r.on} {hf[1].r.on}" == "1.5 2.5 Red Blue false true", "kinds: {hf[0].r.x} {hf[1].r.c}");
}
